From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 20:12:50 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] media: add the Darwin UML Virtio video decoder

The stateful V4L2 decoder for the Darwin host's Virtio video device.

A buffer queued as V4L2_MEMORY_DMABUF is described by the imported dma-buf's
own scatter list, whose entries may have no struct page, so its mapped DMA
addresses are the only valid ones. A host-visible virtio buffer has no
guest-physical address at all: when its exporter names a shared-memory
region, the resource entry carries that region's ID and the plane's offset
inside it, which the negotiated VIRTIO_VIDEO_F_RESOURCE_SHM_REGION feature
permits. V4L2 carries no per-plane offset on a capture buffer, so planes
that share one dma-buf follow one another at their negotiated sizes.

A plane is bounded by its dma-buf, not by its own length, which a client
may state as that plane's size alone while the planes share one buffer. An
imported buffer keeps the scatter-list implementation's cookie and user
count even when the queue's own buffers are host-visible blobs: its scatter
list and attachment belong to that implementation alone, and reading the
blob allocator's state for them made every import fail.

A capture buffer that comes back as an error or as the last one takes the
timestamp the device answered with, as one holding a picture does, so a
client can tell which frame failed and not read the stale one the buffer
carried from its last use.

The wire names the H.264 levels 5.2 to 6.2 and the Constrained High
profile, as it names Constrained Baseline, and the driver maps each to
its V4L2 menu index. A level or profile the table lacks reads as the
first entry of its menu, so a device that lists Constrained High or level
5.2 would be taken for Baseline or level 1.0.

UML removes a device from an exitcall while other CPUs still run tasks, and
a management-console halt, an unbind or a lost vhost-user backend does so
without device_shutdown(). Removal therefore resets the device and deletes
its queues before it returns anything the device held, since a vhost-user
reset does not reach the backend, answers every pending command as failed
so that a queued buffer returns to vb2 in error, and flushes the event work.
The last reference to the video device, perhaps a later close, frees the
driver state.

A synchronous command waits five seconds for its answer, but a device
that answers later still writes the answer into the buffer it was given.
That buffer is therefore memory the command owns, inline or allocated
with it, and the waiter copies a timely answer to its caller. A waiter
that gives up leaves the command, with its request, data and response, to
the late answer or to removal to free, and no response callback runs for
it. An open that fails after the device created its stream destroys that
stream.

A buffer's scatter list takes 16 bytes for every run of pages the buffer
has, so one of tens of megabytes in scattered pages is hundreds of
kilobytes, which a guest with fragmented memory cannot always allocate
in one piece: a 4K HEVC decode on a 1536 MiB Android guest failed to
queue its input buffer with an order 6 allocation failure. The list is
now the payload of the attach command, kept as chunks of a page that are
a descriptor each, so no allocation is larger than a page, and the
command waits for the ring room all its descriptors need. A list too
long for the ring (about 32500 entries) is refused when the buffer is
queued. A device without non-contiguous resources sized a plane's list
for no entry and wrote its one past that; it is sized for the one.

TRY_FMT of a raw capture format answers with the layout S_FMT and G_FMT
report, which is the one the device states: rows at the pitch a guest
allocator gives a buffer the host GPU samples, 16 bytes for NV12, P010
and packed samples and 32 for planar luma, with plane heights unchanged.
A client that compares TRY_FMT with the format it sets, as Chromium's
V4L2 decoder does, no longer sees different strides for a width that is
not a multiple of the pitch.
