+Darwin UML stateful decoder
+
+Adapted from OpenSynergy's Virtio video driver in Android common-modules
+virtual-device commit 4733665ecb4a3adc05f27a59779ee7e1bb247420
+(virtio_video/ and include/uapi/linux/virtio_video.h).
+
+The local decoder-only port uses Linux 6.18 media APIs and the paired
+UML_VIRTIO_VIDEO_VERSION 1 host protocol. It exposes H.264, HEVC, and
+baseline JPEG with hardware-dependent profiles, including 10-bit output.
+The canonical wire declarations are kernel/common/video/virtio_video.h
+in the owning Darwin host repository.
+
+A buffer reaches the device in a RESOURCE_ATTACH command with one scatter list
+per plane, and a list is as long as the buffer has physically separate pieces:
+a 64 MiB buffer in a guest that has run for days is sixteen thousand entries of
+16 bytes. The driver builds the list in chunks of a page, one descriptor
+each, and never asks the kernel for it in one block, which a fragmented guest
+cannot give. A list longer than one command carries (about 32500 entries, a
+buffer of almost 127 MiB in single pages) is refused with EINVAL. The raw
+CAPTURE TRY_FMT answers with the layout S_FMT sets, the pitch the device pads
+its rows to, so a client that sizes buffers from TRY_FMT allocates the rows the
+device writes.
+
+A V4L2_MEMORY_DMABUF capture buffer may be any dma-buf: one the decoder's own
+queue exported, guest memory from a heap or another driver, or one holding all
+of a buffer's planes, which follow one another at their negotiated sizes. The
+lengths a client states for the planes may be those sizes, the dma-buf's size
+or zero, because only the dma-buf bounds where the planes lie.
